/* ~/Library/KeyBindings/DefaultKeyBinding.dict

These bindings make the text cursor keys of Cocoa apps behave as they do in GTK
text widgets on a GNOME desktop. An app reads this file when it launches, so a
change takes effect in an app only after the app restarts.

Each binding here replaces the macOS default for the same key, which lives in
/System/Library/Frameworks/AppKit.framework/Resources/StandardKeyBinding.dict.
A key not bound here keeps its macOS default. That is deliberate for these:

  Option+Left/Right   Move by word. GTK text widgets do not bind these.
  Option+Up/Down      Move by paragraph. GTK text widgets do not bind these.
  Command+Left/Right  Move to the start or end of the line, the same as Home/End.
  Command+Up/Down     Move to the start or end of the document.

Chromium apps such as Chrome and Slack honor these bindings in plain text
fields. They do not reach a field whose page script handles the key itself.
hammerspoon/init.lua covers those apps.

Mission Control claims the Control+arrow chords by default. macos/hotkeys.sh
turns those shortcuts off so that the chords reach apps.


File format

The file is an old-style property list. Each key is zero or more modifier
characters followed by one key character. Each value is a list of selectors,
which run in order.


Modifier characters

  ^   Control
  $   Shift
  ~   Option
  @   Command
  #   Numeric keypad


Key characters, as AppKit reports them

  \UF700  Up Arrow
  \UF701  Down Arrow
  \UF702  Left Arrow
  \UF703  Right Arrow
  \UF704  F1, counting up to \UF726 for F35
  \UF728  Forward Delete (fn+Delete on a Mac keyboard)
  \UF729  Home
  \UF72B  End
  \UF72C  Page Up
  \UF72D  Page Down
  \UF746  Help, which is what a PC keyboard's Insert key reports
  \U0003  Enter on the numeric keypad
  \U0009  Tab
  \U000D  Return
  \U001B  Escape
  \U007F  Backspace, the key labeled Delete on a Mac keyboard

Arrow keys report the numeric keypad modifier on their own. A binding does not
need "#" to match them.


Selectors

The full list is at
https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appkit/nsstandardkeybindingresponding
These are the ones most useful for cursor keys.

Moving by character or line of the display:
  moveBackward  moveForward  moveLeft  moveRight  moveUp  moveDown

Moving within a line of the display, as wrapped on screen:
  moveToBeginningOfLine  moveToEndOfLine
  moveToLeftEndOfLine    moveToRightEndOfLine

Moving within a paragraph, which ends at a line break in the text:
  moveToBeginningOfParagraph  moveToEndOfParagraph

Moving by word:
  moveWordBackward  moveWordForward  moveWordLeft  moveWordRight

Moving by page or to the ends of the document:
  pageUp  pageDown  moveToBeginningOfDocument  moveToEndOfDocument

Scrolling without moving the cursor:
  scrollPageUp  scrollPageDown
  scrollToBeginningOfDocument  scrollToEndOfDocument

Extending the selection. Every movement above except scrolling has a variant
with "AndModifySelection" appended, such as moveWordLeftAndModifySelection.
Paragraphs also have these, with no plain variant:
  moveParagraphBackwardAndModifySelection
  moveParagraphForwardAndModifySelection

Deleting:
  deleteBackward  deleteForward  deleteWordBackward  deleteWordForward
  deleteToBeginningOfLine       deleteToEndOfLine
  deleteToBeginningOfParagraph  deleteToEndOfParagraph

"Left" and "Right" selectors follow the screen. "Backward" and "Forward"
selectors follow reading order. The two differ only in right-to-left text.
*/
{
  /* Home and End move to the start and end of the line. macOS scrolls to the
     ends of the document instead. */
  "\UF729"    = (moveToBeginningOfLine:);                       // Home
  "\UF72B"    = (moveToEndOfLine:);                             // End
  "$\UF729"   = (moveToBeginningOfLineAndModifySelection:);     // Shift+Home
  "$\UF72B"   = (moveToEndOfLineAndModifySelection:);           // Shift+End

  /* Control+Home and Control+End move to the start and end of the document. */
  "^\UF729"   = (moveToBeginningOfDocument:);                   // Control+Home
  "^\UF72B"   = (moveToEndOfDocument:);                         // Control+End
  "^$\UF729"  = (moveToBeginningOfDocumentAndModifySelection:); // Control+Shift+Home
  "^$\UF72B"  = (moveToEndOfDocumentAndModifySelection:);       // Control+Shift+End

  /* Control+Left moves to the start of a word and Control+Right to the end of
     one. These are the same selectors as the Option+Left/Right default. */
  "^\UF702"   = (moveWordLeft:);                                // Control+Left
  "^\UF703"   = (moveWordRight:);                               // Control+Right
  "^$\UF702"  = (moveWordLeftAndModifySelection:);              // Control+Shift+Left
  "^$\UF703"  = (moveWordRightAndModifySelection:);             // Control+Shift+Right

  /* Control+Up moves to the start of the paragraph, or to the start of the one
     before when the cursor is already there. Control+Down mirrors it at the
     end. These are the same selectors as the Option+Up/Down default. With
     Shift, they select to the same places. The paragraph selectors behind
     Option+Shift+Up/Down are not used, because Chromium moves those by a line
     and keeps the column. */
  "^\UF700"   = (moveBackward:, moveToBeginningOfParagraph:);   // Control+Up
  "^\UF701"   = (moveForward:, moveToEndOfParagraph:);          // Control+Down
  "^$\UF700"  = (moveBackwardAndModifySelection:,
                 moveToBeginningOfParagraphAndModifySelection:); // Control+Shift+Up
  "^$\UF701"  = (moveForwardAndModifySelection:,
                 moveToEndOfParagraphAndModifySelection:);      // Control+Shift+Down

  /* Page Up and Page Down move the cursor by a page. macOS scrolls and leaves
     the cursor where it was. */
  "\UF72C"    = (pageUp:);                                      // Page Up
  "\UF72D"    = (pageDown:);                                    // Page Down
  "$\UF72C"   = (pageUpAndModifySelection:);                    // Shift+Page Up
  "$\UF72D"   = (pageDownAndModifySelection:);                  // Shift+Page Down

  /* Control+Backspace and Control+Delete delete a word. With Shift, they
     delete to the start or end of the paragraph. */
  "^\U007F"   = (deleteWordBackward:);                          // Control+Backspace
  "^\UF728"   = (deleteWordForward:);                           // Control+Delete
  "^$\U007F"  = (deleteToBeginningOfParagraph:);                // Control+Shift+Backspace
  "^$\UF728"  = (deleteToEndOfParagraph:);                      // Control+Shift+Delete
}
